WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.768
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.068 --> 00:00:09.942
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:16.633
Unit sixteen: Satirical Writing. Today you will use understatement and litotes such as not unlike and hardly.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:19.643
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.821
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:25.835
Satire.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.044
Satire means writing that uses humour to criticise people or society.

8
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.859
The satire mocked the greedy minister.

9
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.963
Your turn. Say: Satire.

10
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.998
Satire.

11
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:42.496
Irony.

12
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:48.954
Irony means saying the opposite of what you mean, or a surprising difference between expectation and result.

13
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.258
The irony was that the fire station burned down.

14
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.259
Your turn. Say: Irony.

15
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.101
Irony.

16
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.339
Exaggeration.

17
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.393
Exaggeration means making something seem bigger or worse than it is.

18
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:10.260
Exaggeration made the politician's promises look silly.

19
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.723
Your turn. Say: Exaggeration.

20
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.800
Exaggeration.

21
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.120
Social commentary.

22
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:25.229
Social commentary means writing that comments on problems in society.

23
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:28.281
The cartoon is sharp social commentary.

24
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:31.942
Your turn. Say: Social commentary.

25
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.440
Social commentary.

26
00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:38.138
Parody.

27
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.984
Parody means an imitation of a work or style made to be funny.

28
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:46.198
The parody copied the news style perfectly.

29
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:49.250
Your turn. Say: Parody.

30
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:52.950
Parody.

31
00:01:53.717 --> 00:01:54.381
Wit.

32
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.336
Wit means clever, quick humour.

33
00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.100
Her wit made the audience laugh and think.

34
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:03.276
Your turn. Say: Wit.

35
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:06.891
Wit.

36
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.139
Target of satire.

37
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:15.598
Target of satire means the person, idea or institution that a satire criticises.

38
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:18.682
The target of the satire was bureaucracy.

39
00:02:19.283 --> 00:02:22.539
Your turn. Say: Target of satire.

40
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.028
Target of satire.

41
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:29.005
Critical humor.

42
00:02:30.017 --> 00:02:34.152
Critical humor means funny writing that also judges or exposes something.

43
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.715
Critical humor can change opinions.

44
00:02:37.317 --> 00:02:40.213
Your turn. Say: Critical humor.

45
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:44.195
Critical humor.

46
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.779
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:49.617 --> 00:02:55.891
Visal, a satirist writes 'The mayor's speech was not unlike a lullaby.' What is the effect of 'not unlike'?

48
00:02:56.983 --> 00:03:10.903
It is litotes: a positive idea expressed by negating its opposite. Not unlike means similar. Not without charm means with some charm. It is polite on the surface but sharp.

49
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:13.111
And hardly?

50
00:03:14.217 --> 00:03:27.913
Hardly, scarcely and barely are almost negative. 'The plan was hardly a success' means it was a failure. They add understatement, and with ever they can create irony: hardly ever honest.

51
00:03:29.017 --> 00:03:30.331
Any grammar warning?

52
00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:41.337
Do not add another negative: not 'It was hardly not funny'. After hardly at the start of a sentence, invert: Hardly had he spoken when the crowd laughed.

53
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.116
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:49.782
Grammar focus: Understatement: not unlike and not without and hardly.

55
00:03:50.082 --> 00:03:51.327
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.017 --> 00:03:54.353
The mayor's speech was not unlike a lullaby.

57
00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.250
The plan was hardly a success.

58
00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.055
His defence was not without a certain comic effect.

59
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:10.678
Litotes expresses a positive with a negative: not unlike = similar. Hardly, scarcely and barely are near-negatives, so do not add another negative.

60
00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.835
Be careful! Don't say: It was hardly not funny.

61
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.649
Say: It was hardly funny.

62
00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:21.238
Do not add a second negative after hardly.

63
00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:24.407
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.782
The Art of Saying Less.

65
00:04:27.082 --> 00:04:34.015
Satire uses wit and exaggeration to expose the target of satire in a way that can make readers smile and think.

66
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.111
A parody that copies a news report may be not unlike the original, which makes the joke work.

67
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.199
Irony is often quiet, as when a writer calls an empty promise hardly a surprise.

68
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.357
Good social commentary is not without risk, because powerful people dislike being laughed at.

69
00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:57.456
Critical humor works best when the target is clear and the facts are accurate.

70
00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:02.615
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.519
The plan was blank a success.

72
00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:09.682
A, hardly. B, not hardly. C, never hardly.

73
00:05:13.233 --> 00:05:15.256
The answer is A: hardly.

74
00:05:16.417 --> 00:05:19.693
Humour that criticises people or society is blank.

75
00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:23.949
A, satire. B, appendix. C, syntax.

76
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.395
The answer is A: satire.

77
00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.817
Which sentence uses litotes correctly?

78
00:05:33.117 --> 00:05:39.649
A, The joke was not unfunny.. B, The joke was hardly not funny.. C, The joke not was unfunny..

79
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.861
The answer is A: The joke was not unfunny..

80
00:05:47.017 --> 00:05:50.154
An imitation of a style made to be funny is a blank.

81
00:05:50.454 --> 00:05:54.084
A, canon. B, premise. C, parody.

82
00:05:57.633 --> 00:05:59.900
The answer is C: parody.

83
00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.861
According to the reading, what must be clear for critical humor to work?

84
00:06:05.161 --> 00:06:10.080
A, the target and the facts. B, the title only. C, the length.

85
00:06:13.633 --> 00:06:16.469
The answer is A: the target and the facts.

86
00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:21.039
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:21.883 --> 00:06:26.681
Why might satire be an effective way to criticize powerful people or institutions?

88
00:06:35.483 --> 00:06:37.030
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.330 --> 00:07:01.826
Satire can be effective because humour lowers people's defences. A joke can say something that a direct complaint cannot, and exaggeration or irony makes a problem easy to see and remember. It is also not without safety, since the writer can hide behind wit. However, satire works only when the facts behind the joke are accurate and fair.

90
00:07:02.126 --> 00:07:04.044
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:11.527
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and understatement: not unlike and not without and hardly.

92
00:07:11.827 --> 00:07:16.905
Today's value is media literacy. Ask who made the message, and why, before you believe it.

93
00:07:17.205 --> 00:07:19.858
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.158 --> 00:07:24.110
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

